      </p>
      <h3>MP4 vs. WebM</h3>
      <p>
        WebM is an open, royalty-free format developed by Google. It's efficient and used heavily by YouTube internally. However, compatibility can be an issue on older devices or Apple products. MP4's H.264 codec has near-universal hardware decoding support.
      </p>
      <h3>MP4 vs. MKV</h3>
      <p>
        MKV (Matroska) is a highly flexible container that can hold almost any audio/video codec. It's popular for high-quality movie rips. However, MP4 enjoys better native support on mobile devices and smart TVs.
      </p>
      <h3>MP4 vs. AVI</h3>
      <p>
        AVI is an older Microsoft format. While it's still playable, it lacks modern features like efficient compression and subtitle embedding that MP4 offers. For online video, MP4 is unquestionably superior.
      </p>

